TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction, Primary mission"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction, Number of spacecraft; Table 1"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"1 Introduction, Users"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"2.2 Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, Table 4"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, Manoeuvres"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications, Table 5"},{"id":"R8","met":"yes","where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"2.3 Orbit, 2.4, Appendix A (STK 11)"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Figures 1, 3, 4; Appendix A"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography; body text"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","issue":"510 s of 771 s is about 66 percent of a pass, not 60 percent. Using 66 percent gives about 38 min/day, changing the 34-min figure and the downlink margin."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit (eclipse statement) and Table 1","issue":"A 34-minute eclipse implies a beta angle near 10 degrees. An 08:45 node time gives roughly 28 min by estimate (beta about 49 degrees). Verify in STK or correct the figure."},{"where":"Table 6 vs Table 3 and 1 Introduction","issue":"One 60 km x 1,000 km strip per day gives about 6 days to image the 344,400 km2 reef, conflicting with the 45-day nadir mosaic and the reef-coverage narrative. The daily imaging assumption is not reconciled with track geometry."},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL, Table 7","issue":"Lifetime is estimated as scale height divided by a constant decay rate using one density, which is crude. Decay rates change strongly with altitude, so the 32-year and 2.2-year figures need numerical propagation or a caveat."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications, Table 6","issue":"The 50 Mbit/s X-band downlink margin is presented as adequate without a link budget. This is acknowledged but weakens the conclusion."},{"where":"Appendix A, Table A1","issue":"The facility file extension is given as .f; STK facility files normally use .fac. Check that the submitted files open in STK."}],"overall":"Comprehensive, mostly accurate mission design with verified orbit, launch and drag calculations and strong structure. Deductions for numerical inconsistencies (contact fraction, eclipse time, revisit versus daily area), an unclosed link budget, some uncited claims and informal phrasing.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"All four required questions are answered with justification: mission need is built from the limits of existing bleaching monitoring, single-satellite choice is argued, and operator and customers are named. References [1]-[6] support the rationale. Minor gaps: some claims are uncited (reliance of humans on reefs, government funding), and the tone is informal in places.","evidence":[{"where":"1 Introduction, Primary mission","quote":"The primary mission of ZYZZsat is to image the whole Great Barrier Reef in visible bands at 5 m resolution"},{"where":"1 Introduction, Number of spacecraft","quote":"One satellite is therefore used."},{"where":"1 Introduction, Users","quote":"The operator is the Australian Institute of Marine Science (AIMS), which already runs the long-term monitoring program for the reef"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":48.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Strong, largely verified calculations: launch azimuth, J2 inclination, 771 s maximum pass, access statistics, drag decay and data budget all check out. Accuracy is reduced by errors and inconsistencies: 510 s of 771 s is about 66 percent, not 60; the 34-minute eclipse looks high for an 08:45 node; the 60,000 km² daily area conflicts with the 45-day mosaic; the link budget is unclosed.","evidence":[{"where":"2.2 Launch","quote":"The baseline is an SSMS rideshare flight on Vega C from Kourou in early 2025"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit, Inclination","quote":"This agrees with the STK value of 97.7924°."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"so about 60 percent of the contact time, or 34 minutes per day, is counted."},{"where":"2.3 Orbit","quote":"it is in the Earth's shadow for about 34 minutes of each orbit"},{"where":"Table 6 (2.4)","quote":"One strip over the reef: 60 km × 1,000 km"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit, Table 3","quote":"A complete map of the reef directly from overhead takes about 45 days."},{"where":"2.4 Ground Control & Communications","quote":"A full link budget for the X-band downlink has not been calculated in this report"},{"where":"2.5 Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"Without the sail the satellite would stay in orbit for about 32 years"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":9.0,"scale":10.0,"points":9.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions are specific and tied to the evidence: revisit times, pass statistics, downlink margin and decay lifetime are restated with numbers. Four named limitations are identified with consequences and next steps. Minor weakness: the claim that managers prefer weekly coverage is uncited.","evidence":[{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"ZYZZsat meets the mission needs set out in the introduction with one 50 kg satellite."},{"where":"3 Conclusion","quote":"the 45-day complete map is slower than the weekly coverage that reef managers would prefer"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":16.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear report structure with executive summary, numbered sections, numbered and captioned tables and figures, and consistent IEEE-style references. Weaknesses: informal phrasing in places, some uncited claims, and a downlink margin given as about three times in the summary but 3.5 in Section 2.4.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The design is supported by STK simulations and by hand calculations of the orbit, coverage, data budget and orbital decay."},{"where":"1 Introduction","quote":"but this is not even the full extent of their nurture"},{"where":"2.3 Orbit, Figure 2","quote":"Figure 2: Inclination required for a sun-synchronous orbit as a function of altitude."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"this gives about three times the downlink capacity the imaging plan needs"}]}]}
